faq:
  - q: "Do LinkedIn recommendations matter for job search?"
    a: "Yes — three to five strong, specific recommendations increase profile credibility and recruiter click-through rates. They signal that real people with real names are willing to publicly vouch for your work. Generic recommendations ('great to work with, highly recommend') have minimal impact. Specific ones — 'delivered the API infrastructure rewrite that reduced latency by 40% under a 6-week deadline, while coaching two junior engineers' — matter significantly more."
  - q: "Who should I ask for LinkedIn recommendations?"
    a: "In priority order: (1) Former managers who can speak to your level, scope, and impact. (2) Senior peers or cross-functional collaborators who can describe your work from a different angle. (3) Direct reports if you managed people — a strong subordinate recommendation is powerful for management roles. Avoid recommendations from friends or family with no professional context. Avoid generic peer recommendations that say nothing specific."
  - q: "How do I request a LinkedIn recommendation?"
    a: "Be specific about what you want them to cover. Example: 'If you're open to it, would love a recommendation focused on [specific project or responsibility]. Specifically, I'd love it if you could mention [specific outcome or quality]. Happy to return the favor if helpful.' Give them a draft if they ask or seem hesitant — most people appreciate the help and the recommendation is better for it."
  - q: "How many LinkedIn recommendations should I have?"
    a: "Three to five substantive, specific recommendations is the target. More is better but with diminishing returns. One strong recommendation from a VP or C-suite leader who speaks to your specific impact is worth more than five generic peer endorsements. Quality and specificity matter more than quantity."
  - q: "What should a LinkedIn recommendation include?"
    a: "Three things: (1) A specific project, initiative, or period where you worked together. (2) What specifically you contributed — actions, decisions, outcomes. (3) A quantified or observable result. Example structure: 'I worked with [Name] on [X]. They were specifically responsible for [Y]. The outcome was [Z]. I would recommend them for [type of role] because [specific quality].'"
  - q: "Should I write a draft for my recommender?"
    a: "Yes, if they ask or seem uncertain. Most professionals are willing to write a recommendation but unsure what to say. Providing a draft — or specific bullet points of what to cover — makes it easier for them and produces a stronger result. The draft is their starting point, not a script — most will personalize it in their own voice."
